				case 17:
					<section>
						<p class="kicker">GROUNDING, GRADED BY A COMPILER</p>
						<h2 id="scene-title">Three migrations of real Enterprise COBOL</h2>
						<p class="rt-intro">This is the monthly-interest calculation from <strong>AWS CardDemo</strong> — genuine IBM Enterprise COBOL, not a toy. Field layouts come from copybooks <code>CVTRA01Y</code> and <code>CVTRA02Y</code>; the arithmetic is <code>CBACT04C</code> paragraph <code>1300-COMPUTE-INTEREST</code>, unchanged. The same model migrates it three times with increasing grounding: <strong>none</strong>, <strong>static-analysis artifacts</strong>, then <strong>facts resolved by the COBOL compiler</strong>. Every answer is executed against output captured from the real program. <strong>No model grades anything.</strong></p>

						<div class="rt-box">
							<header class="rt-head"><span class="rt-step">!</span><div><strong>Why a COBOL compiler, and why the IBM dialect</strong><small>the point of the whole tab</small></div></header>
							<div style="padding:14px">
								<p class="rt-intro">A model has read enormous amounts of C and very little COBOL — and almost none of it <em>Enterprise</em> COBOL, because that code lives inside banks and insurers and never reaches the public internet. But the real problem is not fluency. <strong>Some COBOL meaning is not in the source text at all.</strong></p>
								<p class="rt-intro">Look at the one line this tab migrates:</p>
								<pre class="rt-code">COMPUTE WS-MONTHLY-INT = (TRAN-CAT-BAL * DIS-INT-RATE) / 1200</pre>
								<p class="rt-intro">There is no <code>ROUNDED</code>, so the result <strong>truncates</strong>. On a balance of $1,000.00 at 18.99% the exact answer is <strong>15.825</strong> and the program pays <strong>15.82</strong>. A migration that rounds pays <strong>15.83</strong> — one cent, on every account, every month. Nothing in the source says “truncate”. The COBOL standard says it, and the compiler implements it.</p>
								<p class="rt-intro"><strong>The dialect is not cosmetic either.</strong> The same program, the same input, compiled under different dialects:</p>
								<pre class="rt-code">MOVE 50000 TO WS-BIN.   *&gt; PIC 9(4) COMP

default  -&gt; 000000      truncates to 4 decimal digits
ibm      -&gt; 050000      keeps the binary value (TRUNC(BIN))
mf       -&gt; 050000
cobol85  -&gt; 000000</pre>
								<p class="rt-intro">Zero versus fifty thousand, with no error and no warning. So the compiler is only an oracle <strong>once you tell it which COBOL you mean</strong>. Run it under the wrong dialect and it becomes a confident, silent liar. This tab compiles with <code>-std=ibm</code> because the source is IBM Enterprise COBOL.</p>
							</div>
						</div>

						@if (_session?.Migration is { } migration)
